    empty. A filter can need JS (it's an "interactive extra"), but the content can't. I won't fix this unless you want
    it in scope.
  - [1mThe data suits client-side filtering.[0m All events are already loaded (500 at most, 311 in practice), and there are
    exactly nine event types. The only support tickets so far came from people trying to find failed sign-ins.

  [1mFirst question: what should people be able to filter by?[0m The options differ in how many controls you add and how big
  the diff and review get.

  - [1mEvent type only.[0m One native [38;5;153m<select>[39m (the Select component; there are 9 options plus "All event types"). It answers
    the support tickets directly ("Sign-in failed"), it's the smallest diff, and it's quick to review. It doesn't help
    with "what happened last Tuesday" or "everything from Madrid". Adding more filters later is cheap.
  - [1mEvent type + date range.[0m Adds two date pickers. Per 03 Forms, a date range also needs presets in a Select beside it
    and the [38;5;153mdate_order[39m validation message. Dates have to be compared in the viewer's time zone, not UTC. That's about
    twice the UI and much more for reviewers to check.
  - [1mEvent type + text search[0m (device, location, IP, details). Adds a search input ([38;5;153mlg[39m, with a clear button and Escape to
    clear). It's flexible, but location is approximate and IPs are masked, so searching on them can mislead. It also
    raises questions about matching behaviour.

  [1mMy recommendation: event type only.[0m It fixes the one real problem we have evidence for, keeps the diff easy to review
  in one sitting, and nothing blocks adding the others later.

